# Agent Sync Rules — FYP Project

You are a daily execution agent on this project: write code, run tests, and complete tasks from the
roadmap. Architectural decisions are recorded as ADRs in `PROJECT_STATE.md` — follow them, and
escalate rather than change them unilaterally.

---

## Session start (mandatory, first thing)

1. Read `PROJECT_STATE.md` — note **Current task** and check **Blocked?**
2. Open `ROADMAP.md`, find that task, read the description **and its acceptance criterion**
3. Read `CLAUDE.md` for commands, architecture and invariants
4. Then begin

**There are exactly two planning documents:** `ROADMAP.md` (what to build) and `PROJECT_STATE.md`
(where we are and why). `README.md` is thesis-facing; `CLAUDE.md` is agent-facing. Do not create
additional status or guide files — they drift and contradict each other.

---

## Task execution

- One task at a time, completed fully before moving on
- **A task is done when its acceptance criterion has been executed and observed** — not when the
  code is written. If you have not run it, the box stays open
- For tasks with tests: run them and confirm they pass before ticking
- For tasks with manual test plans: write the plan document; do not leave it a stub

## After every completed task

1. Tick the box in `ROADMAP.md`
2. In `PROJECT_STATE.md`: advance **Current task**, reset **Status**, add an **Activity log** row
   (date, agent, one-line summary)
3. Commit: `feat(sprint-N): <task> — <description>`

## When blocked

Set **Blocked?** in `PROJECT_STATE.md` to `Yes — <what and why>` and stop. Do not work around a
blocker by silently changing the design.

---

## Decide independently

Production code for roadmap tasks · new files matching the documented structure · running and
fixing tests · fixing bugs found along the way · comments and docstrings

## Escalate first

Anything inconsistent with an ADR · changes to sprint structure or acceptance criteria · a new
library or technology · unresolvable blockers (rate limits, missing credentials, platform
behaviour) · deviation from the documented directory structure

---

## Invariants — breaking any of these is a bug, not a style choice

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Never remove or bypass SHAP explainability** (ADR-011). It is the project's academic
> contribution. If a task appears to conflict with it, escalate.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **No snake_case feature name may ever reach the popup or dashboard** (ADR-010). Everything passes
> through `format_reason()` and `shared/feature_name_to_human_readable.json`. A new feature without
> a template is an incomplete feature.

> [!WARNING]
> **`feature_columns.json` and `xgboost_phishing.pkl` are written by the same training run and
> never hand-edited.** Hand-editing the JSON caused a 12-vs-8 column mismatch that left the system
> silently running a fallback heuristic.

> [!WARNING]
> **Never silently drop a feature.** `explain_prediction()` must raise on unrecognised keys.
> Filtering the feature vector against `feature_columns` previously discarded every browser signal
> without a word, defeating the project's core multi-signal claim.

> [!WARNING]
> **No silent fallback in a serving deployment** (ADR-016). `_simple_rule_prediction()` is gated
> behind `ESA_ALLOW_FALLBACK=1`.

> [!WARNING]
> **Verdict thresholds:** `> 0.70` phishing, `0.40–0.70` suspicious, `< 0.40` legitimate. Changing
> them requires a new ADR.

> [!NOTE]
> **VirusTotal is corroboration, not a trained feature** (ADR-013, amending ADR-008). VT ingests
> PhishTank, so training on its verdicts would be circular. Call it live with a 5s timeout and TTL
> cache, display it, persist it — and never let it enter `feature_columns.json` or change a verdict.

> [!NOTE]
> **`risk_pct` ≠ `confidence_pct`** (ADR-015, superseding ADR-009).
> `risk_pct = round(p*100)`; `confidence_pct = round(max(p, 1-p)*100)`. No UI may derive one from
> the other.

> [!NOTE]
> **Evaluation numbers are measured, never estimated** or carried over from a prior run.

